"Understanding Boron Chemistry as the Surface Modification and Electrolyte Additive for Co‐Free Lithium‐Rich Layered Oxide - Park - 2024 - Advanced Energy Materials - Wiley Online Library (https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/aenm.202401968)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view0\",\"pattern\":\"Electrode Preparation\"}); Total lines: 689\nL325: a) Full cells cycling performance comparison with b) the corresponding average charge and discharge voltage in the 2.0–4.55 V window at a current rate of C/10. c) Cycling performance comparison with d) the corresponding average charge and discharge voltage in the 2.0–4.55 V window at a current rate of C/3. e) LRLO/graphite full cell performance summary plot. (The numbers refer to the entries in Table 【57†S2】 (Supporting Information).\nL326: Additionally, red numbers indicate Co-containing LRLO cathode materials, while black numbers indicate Co-free LRLO cathode materials.).\nL327: ## 3 Conclusion\nL328: The oxygen redox products in LRLO, including radical anion complexes, readily engage with carbonate-based electrolytes, causing parasitic side reactions that compromise the cathode material's lattice structure and result in a rapid deterioration of electrochemical performance. This investigation employs boron chemistry to design a strategy for stabilizing the electrode-electrolyte interphase, proving effective in alleviating capacity and voltage decay.\nL329: The LBO precursor is synthesized using a polyol process, enabling dry coating on the LRLO surface. Under optimized conditions, a uniform LBO coating layer of 15 nm thickness is achieved, contributing to enhanced cycling stability in LRLO/graphite full cells.\nL330: The improved full cell performance is attributed to the surface modification by LBO, which protects LRLO's lattice oxygen, and reacts with PF_{6}^{−} anions to suppress HF acid generation. This mechanistic understanding is supported by the observed reduction in transition metal dissolution and mitigated interphase corrosion in LBO-coated LRLO. The further incorporation of LiBOB additive, acting as an HF scavenger, results in promising battery performance for industrial-level applications.\nL331: The full cell with LBO-coated LRLO cathode demonstrates an impressive 82% capacity retention after 400 cycles at a current rate of C/3, with an average discharge voltage drop of 0.019 V. This minimal voltage decay presents a significant opportunity for engineering LRLO materials toward achieving long-term cycling stability.\nL332: ## 4 Experimental Section\nL333: #### Sample Preparation\nL334: The pristine Li_{1.222}Ni_{0.349}Mn_{0.651}O_{2} sample (denoted as LRLO) was provided by Umicore. SEM images of the pristine LRLO are presented in Figure 【57†S20a–c】 (Supporting Information), revealing oval-spherical secondary particles with an average size of ≈9 µm. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern was obtained for the structural analysis of the pristine LRLO.\nL335: Rietveld refinement was applied to the collected XRD data to determine the lattice parameters and site occupancies of the pristine LRLO sample, as shown in Figure 【57†S20d,e】 (Supporting Information). The XRD pattern can be matched to the R-3m space group, with lattice parameters a = 2.867(6) Å and c = 14.266(4) Å. The refined occupancies reveal a 6.8% Li/Ni mixing between the Li and TM layers.\nL336: In summary, the LRLO sample exhibits high material purity and a well-organized layered structure with low Li/Ni mixing, establishing it as a reliable baseline material for this study. For coating material synthesis, 0.03 mol of Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) (MW = 50 000) was added to 100 mL of Tetraethylene Glycol (TTEG). 0.015 mol of LiOH·H_{2}O and 0.015 mol of H_{3}BO_{3} were then added into the solution.\nL337: After 2 h of heating the solution at 80 °C, the products were naturally cooled down, followed by washing in ethanol seven times. After removing moisture in an 80 °C vacuum oven for one day, the dried powder was ground finely using a mortar and pestle, and finally, using a ball mill at 500 rpm for 5 h with ethanol to make the particle size smaller. The obtained LBO precursor (2% in a weight ratio) was then mixed with the LRLO cathode in a Thinky Mixer (Thinky Corporation) at 2000 rpm for 10 min.\nL338: The well-mixed powder was transferred into the furnace for calcination under different temperatures and dwell time. After calcination, the powder was ground using a mortar and pestle for ≈10 min.\nL339: #### Electrode Preparation\nL340: To evaluate the electrochemical performance of LBO-coated LRLO, electrodes using the uncoated and LBO-coated LRLO were prepared, with SPC65 (carbon black, TIMCAL Ltd.) as the conductive agent and HSV900 (PVDF, Arkema Inc.) as the binder, in a mass ratio of 80:10:10 and a cathode loading of 3 mAh cm^{−2}. The mixture was then dissolved in a proper amount of N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP, ≥ 99%, Sigma–Aldrich) in a Thinky Mixer to form the slurry.\nL341: The slurry was cast onto Al foil and dried at 80 °C in a vacuum oven overnight, followed by 1 h of drying at elevated temperature of 120 °C. The cathode was punched into discs with a 12.7 mm diameter and a loading of active mass ≈14 mg cm^{−2}. For LRLO/graphite full cells, both CR2032 and pouch cells were assembled. The graphite electrode used in this work was provided by NIMTE with an active material ratio of 94%.\nL342: For CR2032 full cells, the graphite electrode was punched into discs with a 13 mm diameter, and the designed N/P ratio was ≈1.1. For single layer pouch-type full cells, the cathode size was 44 × 30 mm, and the anode size was 45 × 32 mm. For all the cells, Celgard 2325 was used as the separator. 1 m LiPF_{6} in EC: DMC = 3:7 (vol%) was obtained from Gotion, USA, and is denoted as carbonate baseline electrolyte.\nL343: All the coin cells were assembled in the Ar-filled glovebox with moisture control (H_{2}O < 0.5 ppm), and 50 µL of electrolyte was used for each coin cell. The single layer pouch cells were first assembled in the atmosphere without electrolytes. The assembled pouch cells were moved to a heating tray inside the glovebox antechamber and dried at 80 °C overnight under vacuum before the electrolyte injection.\nL344: After drying, the dry pouches were moved inside the Ar-filled glovebox without air exposure, and 500 µL of electrolyte was injected into each cell. The pouch cells with electrolytes were vacuum sealed inside the glovebox and transferred out for further testing.\nL345: #### Electrochemical Performance Evaluation\nL346: \nL347: After assembling, the coin cell (CR2032) and pouch cell full cells were evaluated by cycling at a rate of C/10 (where 1C is 270 mAh g^{−1}) after the formation cycle at a current rate C/20. The electrochemical performances of all the cells were tested either by Neware Battery Test System (Neware Technology Ltd., China) or Arbin BT2000 instruments (Arbin instrument, USA).\nL348: #### Characterizations\nL349: FEI Apreo was applied with 5 kV as the accelerating voltage and 0.1 nA as the beam current for the SEM analysis. A backscattered electron imaging technique was applied to verify the uniformity of the LBO surface modification layer on LRLO, especially for large-area coating uniformity evaluation. The microscope detector was first changed to T1 mode, which was extremely sensitive to backscattered electrons. Then, the accelerated voltage was lowered to detect the surface information.\nL350: The contrast gradually appeared when lowering the accelerated voltage from 5 kV to 200 V, while the optimized voltage was 500 to 1000 V to acquire clear images. A Thermofisher Talos F200X G2 transmission electron microscope, equipped with a Ceta camera and operated at 200 kV, was used to acquire STEM-EELS and STEM-EDS data. To minimize possible electron beam irradiation effects, EELS spectra presented in this work were acquired from areas without pre-beam irradiation.\nL351: ICP-MS analysis was performed with a Thermo iCAP RQ ICP-MS to analyze the elemental concentration in fresh and cycled electrolytes. The ^{11}B, ^{19}F, and ^{31}P-NMR measurements of the electrolyte samples were performed with a JEOL ECA 500 spectrometer. Liquid NMR samples were prepared by adding 10 µL of electrolyte to 600 µL of DMSO-D6 solution, and 50 µL of α, α, α-Trifluorotoluene was added to each sample as the reference and sealed in an NMR tube inside the Ar-filled glovebox for further measurement.\nL352: The NMR spectrums were analyzed with MestReNova. All spectra were calibrated with α, α, α-Trifluorotoluene at −63.72 ppm. FT-IR spectra were collected using Nicolet 6700 Fourier transform infrared spectrometer. Approximately 5−10 mg of LRLO was dried at 80 °C for at least 12 h before the FT-IR analysis.\nL353: The electronic conductivity was determined using the direct current polarization method using a Biologic SP-200 impedance analyzer by applying a bias of 10 mV for 1 h and extracting the steady-state leakage current.\nL354: ## Acknowledgements\nL355: This work was supported by Umicore. The authors thank Wendy Zhou for her invaluable advice. STEM-EELS in this work were performed at the Nano3 of UCSD, a member of the National Nanotechnology Coordinated Infrastructure, which is supported by the National Science Foundation (Grant ECCS-1542148). The ICP-MS was conducted at the Environmental and Complex Analysis Laboratory (ECAL) in the Chemistry and Biochemistry department at UC San Diego.\nL356: The NMR in this work was conducted at the Chemistry NMR facility at UC San Diego. The authors are grateful for Umicore providing the LRLO cathode materials used in the research. The authors thank Prof. Zhaoping Liu's group from Ningbo Institute of Materials Technology & Engineering (NIMTE) for providing the graphite anode. Finally, I dedicate this thesis to my mother.\nL357: ## Conflict of Interest\nL358: \nL359: The authors declare no conflict of interest.\nL360: ## Author Contributions\nL361: N.P., M.Z., and Y.S.M. conceived the idea. N.P. and M.Z. designed the experiments. N.P. synthesized the organic nano-sized boron precursor and performed LBO-coating on LRLO cathode material. N.P. collected and analyzed the data for SEM-EDS, ICP-MS, liquid NMR, FT-IR, and electrochemical performances. B.H. collected and processed the data for STEM-EELS and STEM-EDS. All authors discussed the results and contributed to the manuscript. All authors have approved the final version of the manuscript.\nL362: ## Data Availability Statement\nL363: \nL364: The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.\nL365: ##\nL366: \nL367: Supporting Information\nL368: \nL369: Filename  | Description\nL370: --- | ---\nL371: 【83†aenm202401968-sup-0001-SuppMat.docx】7.5 MB  | Supporting Information\nL372: \nL373: Please note: The publisher is not responsible for the content or functionality of any supporting information supplied by the authors. Any queries (other than missing content) should be directed to the corresponding author for the article.\nL374: ## 【30†References】\nL375: \nL376:   * 1 M. Armand, P. Axmann, D. Bresser, M. Copley, K. Edström, C. Ekberg, D. Guyomard, B. Lestriez, P. Novák, M. Petranikova, W. Porcher, S. Trabesinger, M. Wohlfahrt-Mehrens, H. Zhang, J. Power Sources 2020, 479, 228708.\nL377: \nL378: 【84†CAS】 【85†Web of Science®】 【86†Google Scholar】\nL379: \nL380:   * 2 M. D. Radin, S. Hy, M. Sina, C. Fang, H. Liu, J. Vinckeviciute, M. Zhang, M. S. Whittingham, Y. S. Meng, A. van der Ven, Adv. Energy Mater. 2017, 7, 1602888.\nL381: \nL382: 【87†CAS】 【88†Web of Science®】 【89†Google Scholar】\nL383:   * 3 F. Wu, G. T. Kim, T. Diemant, M. Kuenzel, A. R. Schür, X. Gao, B. Qin, D. Alwast, Z. Jusys, R. J. Behm, D. Geiger, U. Kaiser, S. Passerini, Adv. Energy Mater. 2020, 10, 2001830.\nL384: \nL385: 【90†CAS】 【91†Web of Science®】 【92†Google Scholar】\nL386: \nL387:   * 4 B. Zhang, L. Wang, X. Wang, S. Zhou, A. Fu, Y. Yan, Q. Wang, Q. Xie, D. Peng, Y. Qiao, S. G. Sun, Energy Storage Mater. 2022, 53, 492.\nL388: \nL389: 【93†Web of Science®】 【94†Google Scholar】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://publications.anl.gov/anlpubs/2022/07/176234.pdf)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"pattern\":\"coolant density\"}); Total lines: 4457\nNo matching text found for \"coolant density\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://publications.anl.gov/anlpubs/2022/07/176234.pdf)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"pattern\":\"40 g\"}); Total lines: 4457\nL2613@P100: 𝑁𝐴𝑆𝐼𝐶 = 𝑁𝑠𝑒𝑟𝑖𝑒𝑠/𝑁𝑐𝑒𝑙𝑙𝑠/𝐴𝑆𝐼𝐶 (7.2)\nL2614@P100: The number of BMU components, i, in the pack (Ni/pack) depend on NASIC as follows:\nL2615@P100-101: 𝑁𝑖/𝑝𝑎𝑐𝑘 = 𝑀𝑣𝑒ℎ𝑖𝑐𝑙𝑒[𝑁𝑓𝑖𝑥𝑒𝑑 + (𝑁𝑖/𝐴𝑆𝐼𝐶𝑁𝐴𝑆𝐼𝐶 − 𝑁𝑜𝑓𝑓𝑠𝑒𝑡)] (7.3)83\nL2616@P101: where Nfixed is the fixed number of components needed for a given vehicle, and Ni/ASIC isthe number\nL2617@P101: of each component, i, per ASIC. Noffset is an offset number used to adjust NASIC. This term is\nL2618@P101: generally needed when boards are in a master/slave configuration with one or two of the boards\nL2619@P101: controlling the others. The term in the parentheses is rounded up to the nearest whole number.\nL2620@P101: Finally, Mvehicle is a multiplier for a given vehicle type. Mvehicle equals one for all components in\nL2621@P101: the BMU. Its value will change for some BDU components (see next sub-section). Table 7.1\nL2622@P101: provides the values of each term for each component in the BMU.\nL2623@P101: Table 7.1. Reference costs and amounts for components in the battery management unit (BMU).\nL2624@P101: Component\nL2625@P101: Cost,\nL2626@P101: $/unit\nL2627@P101: (Co)\nL2628@P101: Volume\nL2629@P101: Basis for\nL2630@P101: Cost (N0)\nL2631@P101: p\u0002Value\nL2632@P101: Fixed\nL2633@P101: Number\nL2634@P101: (Nfixed)\nL2635@P101: # per\nL2636@P101: ASIC\nL2637@P101: (Ni/ASIC)\nL2638@P101: Offset\nL2639@P101: per\nL2640@P101: ASCI\nL2641@P101: (Noffset)\nL2642@P101: Vehicle\nL2643@P101: Multiplier\nL2644@P101: (Mvehicle)\nL2645@P101: PCB 5.00 1,000 0.9 1 0 0 1\nL2646@P101: Connector-20 pin 1.58 5,000 0.9 1 0.5 0 1\nL2647@P101: Connector-16 pin 1.30 5,000 0.9 1 0 0 1\nL2648@P101: Conformal coating 0.50 1,000 0.9 2 0.166 0 1\nL2649@P101: Main micro 20.30 500 0.9 1 0 0 1\nL2650@P101: Piezoelectric crystal 0.11 10,000 0.9 1 1 0 1\nL2651@P101: Flash memory 0.37 25,000 0.9 1 0 0 1\nL2652@P101: V sense micro 3.95 2,000 0.9 0 1 0 1\nL2653@P101: V sense ASIC 2.95 1,000 0.9 0 1 0 1\nL2654@P101: CAN transceiver 0.52 12,500 0.9 4 0 0 1\nL2655@P101: Common mode choke 0.64 12,000 0.9 3 0 0 1\nL2656@P101: 5V ref-main 0.78 5,000 0.9 0 1 0 1\nL2657@P101: 5V reference - Vsense 1.89 500 0.9 1 0 0 1\nL2658@P101: Dual isolators 1.09 1,000 0.9 0 1 -2 1\nL2659@P101: Single optocouplers 1.58 3,000 0.9 0 0.5 0 1\nL2660@P101: Buffers 0.08 125,000 0.9 0 0.3333 0 1\nL2661@P101: Schottky diode 0.70 5,000 0.9 1 0 0 1\nL2662@P101: Diodes 0.04 75,000 0.9 26 26 0 1\nL2663@P101: Resistors-0603 0.01 50,000 0.9 51 45 0 1\nL2664@P101: Resistors-0805 0.01 50,000 0.9 51 45 0 1\nL2665@P101: Resistors-1206 0.32 1,000 0.9 8 20 0 1\nL2666@P101: Electrolytic capacitor 0.04 100,000 0.9 2 1 0 1\nL2667@P101: Capacitor-0603 0.002 15,000 0.9 26 31 0 1\nL2668@P101: Capacitor-0805 0.01 4,000 0.9 25 30 0 1\nL2669@P101: Capacitor-1206 0.04 4,000 0.9 12 2 0 1\nL2670@P101: Transistors 0.08 30,000 0.9 14 14 0 1\nL2671@P101: Resistor jumper 0.004 125,000 0.9 2 0 0 1\nL2672@P101: Contactor control 10.00 1 1 1 0 0 1\nL2673@P101-102: BMU enclosure 3.00 1 1 1 0 0 184\nL2674@P102: Table 7.1 also provides reference values used to calculate the cost of each component. The table\nL2675@P102: includes a reference cost (C0) for a given reference manufacturing volume (N0) and a scaling factor\nL2676@P102: (p), which is used to adjust the cost for the volume of components used per year. The individual\nL2677@P102: cost of each component is calculated using the following equation:\nL2678@P102: 𝐶𝑖 = 𝐶𝑖,0 (\nL2679@P102: 𝑁𝑖,0\nL2680@P102: 𝑁𝑖/𝑝𝑎𝑐𝑘𝑁𝑝𝑎𝑐𝑘/𝑦𝑒𝑎𝑟)\nL2681@P102: 1−𝑝\nL2682@P102: (7.4)\nL2683@P102: In Eq. 7.4, Npack/year is the number of packs manufactured per year, which is set on ‘Dashboard.’\nL2684@P102: For a given BMU component in Table 7.1, its total cost within the pack is calculated by multiplying\nL2685@P102: Eq. 7.3 and Eq. 7.4. The total BMU cost per pack is the sum of all component costs.\nL2686@P102: BatPaC does not set the mass and volume of each component in the BMU. Instead, the mass and\nL2687@P102: volume are calculated based on a set value for each printed circuit board (PCB) plus an additional\nL2688@P102: amount for each ASIC. This methodology ensures the mass and volume of the BMU increase with\nL2689@P102: increasing complexity of the system. The PCB mass and volume have default values of 40 g and\nL2690@P102: 0.16 L, respectively. The default additions per ASIC are 20 g and 0.08 L. An additional 10% of\nL2691@P102: the total BMU mass/volume is included in the total BMS mass/volume to account for the enclosure\nL2692@P102: around the BMU. Note that the calculation also increases the mass and volume of the PCB based\nL2693@P102: on the number of main contactors in the system (see next sub-section). This is used as a proxy to\nL2694@P102: increase the size of the BMU for larger, higher-voltage packs. For packs with one module or <50\nL2695@P102: V, the PCB mass/volume and the contactor control cost in Table 7.1 are not included in the BMU.\nL2696@P102: These values are excluded to reflect the decrease in complexity of the BMU for smaller packs.\nL2697@P102: 7.3 Battery Disconnect Unit and Power Electronics\nL2698@P102: The battery disconnect unit (BDU) and power electronics are essential parts of any electrified\nL2699@P102: vehicle. These units are responsible for transitioning between discharge and charge modes and\nL2700@P102: cutting off power to/from the battery for repair or in emergencies. The specific components will\nL2701@P102: change between different vehicle types.\nL2702@P102: The number and cost of the components are calculated in a similar manner to the BMU components\nL2703@P102: (i.e., equations 7.3 and 7.4). The major difference is that none of the BDU or power electronics\nL2704@P102: components depend on the number of ASIC or include an offset – i.e., Ni/ASIC and Noffset equal zero\nL2705@P102: in Eq. 7.3. Table 7.2 lists the BDU and power electronics components and provides the values for\nL2706@P102: Nfixed and Mvehicle. Mvehicle equals one for all components except the auxiliary contactor and the\nL2707@P102: charger fuse. Their values are one for plug-in hybrid and full electric vehicles (PHEVs and EVs),\nL2708@P102: which undergo charging from an external source, and zero for mild and high-power hybrid vehicles\nL2709@P102: (mildHEV and HEV-HP).\nL2710@P102: Three of the BDU and power electronics components are also adjusted based on the size of the\nL2711@P102: pack. The main contactors (Contactor – main), precharge contactors, and current sensors are\nL2712@P102: excluded in the BMS for small packs – i.e., packs with one module or <50 V. These components\nL2713@P102-103: are either not needed or replaced with a significantly cheaper/smaller component that can be 85\nL2714@P103: neglected in the calculation. For packs between 50 and 100 V, one main contactor is included.\nL2715@P103: Two main contactors are included in packs with >100 V.\nL2716@P103: Table 7.2. Reference costs and amounts for components in the battery disconnect unit and power\nL2717@P103: electronics. Not shown are modifications to the main contactors, precharge contactors, and current\nL2718@P103: sensors for smaller packs (see text for details).\nL2719@P103: Component Base Cost,\nL2720@P103: $/unit (Co)\nL2721@P103: Volume\nL2722@P103: Basis for\nL2723@P103: Cost (No)\nL2724@P103: p\u0002Value\nL2725@P103: 𝑵𝒇𝒊𝒙𝒆𝒅\nL2726@P103: Vehicle\nL2727@P103: Multiplier\nL2728@P103: (Mvehicle)\nL2729@P103: Volume\nL2730@P103: (L/unit)\nL2731@P103: Mass\nL2732@P103: (g/Unit)\nL2733@P103: Contactor - Main Table 7.3 200,000 0.95 2 1 0.3193 750\nL2734@P103: Contactor - Aux 10 100 1 2 0 or 1 0 180\nL2735@P103: Precharge contactor 10 10,000 0.9 1 1 0.125 90\nL2736@P103: Precharge resistor 4.44 1,000 0.9 1 1 0.08 185\nL2737@P103: Service disconnect 1 10,000 0.9 1 1 0.576 150\nL2738@P103: Service disconnect\nL2739@P103: fuse 10 10,000 0.9 1 1 0.0884 80\nL2740@P103: Charger fuse 3 10,000 0.9 1 0 or 1 0.004 24\nL2741@P103: Current sensor 60.33 50 0.9 1 1 0.0365 55\nL2742@P103: The cost of the main contactor is a function of the current and voltage. If the voltage is less than\nL2743@P103: 50V, the main contactor would be replaced by a significantly cheaper switch and its cost is\nL2744@P103: neglected (see previous paragraph). At >50 V, the cost is assumed to scale linearly with peak pack\nL2745@P103: current based on the values in Table 7.3.\nL2746@P103: Table 7.3. Reference costs for determining base cost of the main contactor.\nL2747@P103: Reference for Contactor-Main Base Cost,\nL2748@P103: $/unit (C0)\nL2749@P103: Volume Basis for\nL2750@P103: Cost (N0)\nL2751@P103: p-Value\nL2752@P103: 10A Contactor 10 200,000 0.95\nL2753@P103: 600A Contactor 52.86 200,000 0.95\nL2754@P103: The mass and volume of the BDU and power electronics components are calculated by multiplying\nL2755@P103: the number of each component by their per-unit value (i.e., L/unit or g/unit) in Table 7.2.\nL2756@P103: 7.4 Interfacing Electronics\nL2757@P103: The interfacing electronics include the low and high voltage wiring between the BMU, BDU, and\nL2758@P103: module management system. Its cost will depend on the pack current and is calculated by linearly\nL2759@P103: extrapolating between the two default scenarios in Table 7.4. These scenarios are estimations for\nL2760@P103: a generic pack based on vehicle battery teardown reports. The entire cost is neglected for small\nL2761@P103: packs (one module or < 50 V). BatPaC does not provide a mass for the interfacing electronics\nL2762@P103: because its mass is small and assumed to be accounted for by the other BMS components. For\nL2763@P103: instance, its mass is lumped into the default mass inputs for the BMU (Section 7.2) and module\nL2764@P103: management system (Section 5.3.2). No volume is added to the BMS since the interface electronics\nL2765@P103-104: are assumed to reside within the void spaces of the pack.86\nL2766@P104: Table 7.4. Reference costs for the interface electronics.\nL2767@P104: Reference for Interface Electronics Cost, $/unit Pack Current, A\nL2768@P104: Scenario 1 20 50\nL2769@P104: Scenario 2 60 500\nL2770@P104: 7.5 Module Electronics\nL2771@P104: Figure 7.2 provides a schematic of the module management unit for a single module. The module\nL2772@P104: management unit includes over-charge protection (i.e., sense line fuses), temperature sensors, and\nL2773@P104: a sense printed circuit board (PCB) to prevent abusive operation of the cells. The number of sense\nL2774@P104: line fuses is calculated using the following equation:\nL2775@P104: 𝑁𝑠𝑒𝑛𝑠𝑒 = (\nL2776@P104: 𝑁𝑐𝑒𝑙𝑙𝑠/𝑚𝑜𝑑\nL2777@P104: 𝑁𝑐𝑒𝑙𝑙𝑠,||\nL2778@P104: + 1) 𝑁𝑚𝑜𝑑/𝑝𝑎𝑐𝑘 (7.5)\nL2779@P104: which is rounded to the nearest whole number and corresponds to one fuse for every cell in series\nL2780@P104: in a module plus an additional fuse per module. Two temperature sensors are included per module\nL2781@P104: plus two additional at the pack level. One sense PCB is included for each module. The costs of the\nL2782@P104: module electronics components are determined using Eq. 7.4 with the inputs from Table 7.5. The\nL2783@P104: masses and volumes of these components are not directly calculated. They are included in the\nL2784@P104: mass/volume of the module management system described in Section 5.3.2. The values for the\nL2785@P104-105: module management system account for all these components plus equipment for interfacing the 87\nL2786@P105: cells with the front and back plates, along with connectors from the inside to the outside of the\nL2787@P105: module.\nL2788@P105: Table 7.5. Reference costs for the module electronics.\nL2789@P105: Module Electronic Component Base Cost,\nL2790@P105: $/unit (C0)\nL2791@P105: Volume Basis\nL2792@P105: for Cost (N0)\nL2793@P105: p-Value\nL2794@P105: Sense line fuses 0.33 10,500 0.9\nL2795@P105: Temperature sensors 0.23 1,000 0.95\nL2796@P105: Sense PCB 0.50 1 1\nL2797@P105: Figure 7.2 Schematic of module management unit for a single module\nL2798@P105: 7.6 Total BMS Mass, Volume, and Cost\nL2799@P105: The total BMS mass and volume are calculated by adding the BDU, power electronics, and BMU\nL2800@P105: values (including BMU enclosure) and multiplying by 1.3 to account for further enclosures. The\nL2801@P105: masses for the interface electronics are assumed to be incorporated in the other BMS components.\nL2802@P105: There is no volume addition for the interface electronics since they are assumed to reside within\nL2803@P105: the void space of the pack. The mass/volume of the module electronics are also excluded from\nL2804@P105: these totals because they are already included in the module design (see previous section and\nL2805@P105: Section 5.3.2). The BMS is delocalized in BatPaC, and its mass and volume are added at the pack\nL2806@P105-106: level. No distinction is made on the location of the components since a variety of options have 88\nL2807@P106: been observed in industry – e.g., different amounts of BMU components and module electronics\nL2808@P106: inside and outside of the module and pack have been observed. The cost of the BMS is the sum of\n"
